OSP Web Event Service
Open Service Platform

!!! Introduction

The Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService is a service that allows a web application
server to notify clients running in a browser, using a RFC 6455
WebSocket and a subject-based publish-subscribe mechanism.

The way this service works is explained in the following section.


!!! The WebEvent Protocol

JavaScript code running in the browser will open a WebSocket connection
to the OSP web server (using path "/webevent") and send a single-frame 
text subscription message specifying the subjects to subscribe to. The format 
of the subscription message is:

    SUBSCRIBE <subjectList> WebEvent/1.0
----

where <subjectList> is a comma-separated list of subject names not
containing white space characters. A comma inside brackets ([], () or {})
belongs to the subject name. An empty subject name makes the message invalid.

Subject names use a hierarchical, reverse DNS-style syntax, with the
parts of the subject delimited by periods. Subject names must not
contain spaces and their parts should be valid C++ identifiers. 
However, the latter restriction is not enforced. 
Subject names are hierarchical. A subscriber to
a "parent" subject (e.g., "com.appinf.events") will also receive
all "child" subjects (e.g., "com.appinf.events.someEvent" and 
"com.appinf.events.someOtherEvent").

A client can unsubscribe from specific events by either sending an
unsubscribe request or by simply closing the WebSocket. The
format of the UNSUBSCRIBE message is similar to the SUBSCRIBE message:

    UNSUBSCRIBE <subjectList>|* WebEvent/1.0
----

where <subjectList> is a comma-separated list of subject names, as
with the SUBSCRIBE message. Instead of a subject list, a single
asterisk can be specified, which means unsubscribe from all
subjects.

After the initial SUBSCRIBE message the client can send additional
SUBSCRIBE or UNSUBSCRIBE messages, and thus dynamically change its 
subscriptions without having to re-establish the WebSocket.

Once a client has opened a WebSocket and sent the SUBSCRIBE
message, the WebSocket connection will be "parked" on the server
and only used when an event message needs to be sent to the
client. This allows a very high number of simultaneous subscribers,
since a single subscriber does not consume much server resources,
except for the socket and some data overhead.
Specifically, a specific thread for the WebSocket connection will only be 
used during the initial connection setup.

Notification messages sent to clients are single-frame text
messages with the following format:

    NOTIFY <subjectName> WebEvent/1.0\r\n
    <data>
----

where <subjectName> is the subjectName specified in the
call to notify(), and <data> is the given data string, which
typically should be a valid serialized JSON or XML document in UTF-8 
encoding, although any valid UTF-8 string can be sent.

If the osp.web.event.clientNotify configuration property is true, a client
can also send notifications to other clients by sending a NOTIFY message
to the server. Otherwise such messages are dropped.

A client can send a NOTIFY message using the special subject
"system.ping" to the server, and the server will respond with a 
NOTIFY using the subject "system.pong", whatever the setting of
osp.web.event.clientNotify. This can be used as a heartbeat
mechanism to verify that the connection to the server is alive.
Any data sent with the ping message will be sent back with the pong
message.


!!! Using the WebEventService

The WebEventService is implemented in the <*com.appinf.osp.webevent*> bundle
and registered under the service name "com.appinf.osp.webevent".


!! On the Server (C++)

To obtain the service:

    Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::Ptr pWebEventService = 
        Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder::find<Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService>(pContext);
----

To send a notification to all subscribers to a given subject, use the
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::notify() method, specifying the
subject name and payload. Note that the payload must be an UTF-8 character
sequence - typically a JSON or XML document.

    pWebEventService->notify("sample.event", "Some event data");
----

To receive notifications on a specific subject, use the 
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::subjectNotified() member function, specifying
the subject name. The function will return a reference to a 
Poco::BasicEvent object which can be used to register a delegate
receiving the notification.

    pWebEventService->subjectNotified("sample.event") += Poco::delegate(onEvent);
----

The delegate function then looks like:

    void onEvent(const Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::NotificationEvent& ev)
    {
        const std::string& subject = ev.first;
        const std::string& data = ev.second;
        // ...
    }
----

The Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::NotificationEvent type is simply a 
<[std::pair<std::string, std::string>]>, with the first item containing the
subject name and the second item containing the data.

To send a notification only to the connections of one user, use
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::notifyUser(). The user a connection
belongs to is determined when the connection is established: the user that the
web server has authenticated for the upgrade request (see Authentication and
Authorization below). An application that identifies users in another way
installs a resolver with
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::setUserResolver(), which replaces the
authenticated user, for example by looking up the web session referenced by
the request's cookies:

    pWebEventService->setUserResolver(
        [](const Poco::Net::HTTPServerRequest& request)
        {
            return usernameFromSession(request); // empty if anonymous
        });
    pWebEventService->notifyUser("alice", "sample.event", "Some event data");
----

Connections for which the resolver returned an empty string belong to no user
and are never notified by notifyUser(). Without a resolver, a connection also
belongs to no user if the web server did not authenticate its upgrade request,
or authenticated it with a bearer token that carries a scope: the subject
authorizer and notifyUser() know only the user name, which would give the
connection everything the user may do. A resolver can give such a connection a
user; while it runs, Poco::OSP::Auth::RequestIdentity::current() returns the
authenticated user and scope. Pass nullptr to setUserResolver() to remove the
resolver, for example when the bundle that installed it stops.

To answer a new subscription with the current value of a subject, register a
delegate for the initialDataRequested event. It is fired for each subject a
connection subscribes to; the data the delegate sets is sent as a NOTIFY for
that subject to the subscribing connection only:

    void onInitialDataRequested(Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::InitialDataRequest& request)
    {
        if (request.subject == "sample.value")
        {
            request.data = currentValue();
            request.hasData = true;
        }
    }
----

Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::listSubscribers() returns the user, page
and client address of every connection. The page is the value of the "page"
query parameter of the upgrade request (for example
"/webevent?page=/app/index.html"), or its Referer header if there is no such
parameter; browsers send no Referer with WebSocket upgrade requests.


!! On the Client (JavaScript)

The following JavaScript module (webevent.js) can be used on the client to subscribe to, and receive
event notifications.

    // webevent.js
    
    var WebEvent = 
    {
        webSocket: null,
    
        available: function()
            {
                return "WebSocket" in window;
            },
        
        connect: function()
            {
                if (!WebEvent.available()) return false;

                var wss = window.location.protocol == "https:" ? "wss://" : "ws://";
                WebEvent.webSocket = new WebSocket(wss + window.location.host + "/webevent");    
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onopen = function()      
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onConnect) WebEvent.onConnect();
                }; 
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onmessage = function(evt)      
                {
                    var msg = evt.data;
                    var parsedEvent = WebEvent.parseEvent(msg);
                    if (parsedEvent.method == "NOTIFY")
                    {
                        WebEvent.onNotify(parsedEvent);
                    }
                };
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onclose = function()
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onDisconnect) WebEvent.onDisconnect();
                    WebEvent.webSocket = null;
                };
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onerror = function()
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onError) WebEvent.onError();
                };
            
                return true;
            },
        
        disconnect: function()
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket) 
                {
                    WebEvent.webSocket.close();
                    WebEvent.webSocket = null;
                }
            
            },

        subscribe: function (subject)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "SUBSCRIBE " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0";
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },
      
        unsubscribe: function (subject)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "UNSUBSCRIBE " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0";
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },
        
        notify: function (subject, data)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "NOTIFY " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0\r\n" + data;
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },

        onConnect: function() {},

        onDisconnect: function() {},
    
        onError: function() {},
    
        onNotify: function(evt) {},
    
        parseEvent: function(msg)
            {
                var event = {};
                var i = 0;
                var methodStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.method = msg.substring(methodStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] == ' ') i++;
                var subjectStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.subject = msg.substring(subjectStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] == ' ') i++;
                var versionStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.version = msg.substring(versionStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && (msg[i] == '\r' || msg[i] == '\n')) i++;
                event.data = msg.substring(i);      
                return event;
            }
    };
----

To subscribe to an event, embed the following JavaScript in a web page. The server
admits the WebSocket of a browser only if the browser authenticates the upgrade
request: with the cookie of the web session that osp.web.event.session names, once
the browser has logged in to that session, or with credentials the web server
accepts (see Authentication and Authorization below):

    <script type="text/javascript" src="webevent.js"></script>
    <script>
    if (WebEvent.available())
    {
        WebEvent.onNotify = function(evt)
            {
               var subject = evt.subject;
               var data = evt.data;
               // ...
            };
        
        WebEvent.onConnect = function()
            {
                WebEvent.subscribe("sample.event");
            };

        WebEvent.connect();
    }
    </script>
----


!!! Authentication and Authorization

The <*com.appinf.osp.webevent*> bundle maps the "/webevent" path with the
permission that osp.web.event.permission names and, if osp.web.event.session names
one, a web session, so the OSP web server authenticates and authorizes the
WebSocket upgrade request before the connection is established, as it does for
every request handler that declares a permission:

  - A client sends an Authorization header with the upgrade request: HTTP
    Basic credentials of an account, or a bearer token, which the web server
    checks with its token validator (osp.web.tokenValidatorName). Without
    osp.web.event.session, the server answers an upgrade request without valid
    credentials with 401 and an HTTP Basic challenge (a Bearer challenge if
    osp.web.authMethods admits bearer tokens but not Basic credentials); with
    osp.web.event.session, it answers with 401 and no challenge.
  - A browser is authenticated by the cookie of its web session if
    osp.web.event.session is set to the name of a web session and the browser
    has logged in to that session: a request handler of the application has
    stored the name of the user in the session's "username" property (see
    Session-Based Authentication in the OSP Web documentation), and the user is
    an account of the auth service. The default is empty: no session login. A
    page served by the same server then sends the cookie with the upgrade
    request. A browser that has not logged in, or whose session has ended (by
    logout, by expiry, or by a restart of the server when no session store keeps
    the sessions), is refused, even on a page that itself requires no login. If
    a request carries both a cookie and an Authorization header, the
    Authorization header decides.

The global osp.web.authMethods setting limits which of these methods apply.
An upgrade request without credentials, or with credentials the server does not
accept, is answered with 401 Unauthorized; a request of a user who does not have
the permission is answered with 403 Forbidden. In both cases no WebSocket
connection is established.

A browser does not tell a page why its WebSocket could not be opened: a refused
upgrade request fires the error and close events (close code 1006), as a network
failure does. A page that reconnects its WebSocket should therefore check, with a
request whose HTTP status it can read, whether its session is still logged in, and
send the user to the login page once the server answers that the session has ended;
a check that fails because the server cannot be reached says nothing about the
session. Otherwise the page keeps retrying, and keeps showing the last events it
received as if they were current.

The permission is the setting osp.web.event.permission (see Configuring the
WebEventService below). Beyond that, a delegate of the requestReceived event can
reject a connection for reasons of its own. An empty permission turns the
authentication of the upgrade request off, for applications that authenticate
their clients themselves in such a delegate.


!! Subject Authorizer

The permission decides who may connect. Which subjects a connection may subscribe
to, and notify, is decided by the subject authorizer, a function installed with
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::setSubjectAuthorizer(). It is asked with the
user of the connection (the authenticated user, or the one the resolver installed
with setUserResolver() gave; empty for none), the subject, and the action:

    pWebEventService->setSubjectAuthorizer(
        [](const std::string& username, const std::string& subject,
            Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::SubjectAction action)
        {
            if (subject == "sample" || subject.starts_with("sample.private"))
                return username == "admin";
            return action == Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::SubjectAction::SUBSCRIBE;
        });
----

The authorizer is asked for each subject of a SUBSCRIBE message, and of a NOTIFY
message while osp.web.event.clientNotify is true. A subject it refuses is left out
of the subscription, or not notified; the rest of the message is processed. A
refusal is logged at notice level at most once every five seconds, and at debug
level otherwise, so that a client cannot fill the log. A subject for which the
authorizer throws is refused as well, and logged at warning level, with the same
limit. No initial data is requested for a refused subject. Subscriptions made
before the authorizer is installed are not checked again. What the server
notifies with notify() or notifyUser() is not asked about. Without an authorizer,
every connection may subscribe to and notify every subject; pass nullptr to remove
it, for example when the bundle that installed it stops.

The authorizer runs on the worker threads that read the frames of the clients,
possibly on several at once. It must be thread-safe, and it must not block, for
example on a database or a remote service: a worker that waits holds up the frames
of other clients. An authorizer that needs such data looks it up in advance.

Subjects have ancestors: a subscription to "sample" receives what is notified for
"sample.private.data" as well. The two actions treat them differently:

  - SUBSCRIBE: the authorizer is asked about the subject of the
    subscription only. An authorizer that protects a subject must therefore also
    refuse every ancestor of it, up to the first part of the name, to a user who
    may not see it, as the example above does for "sample".
  - NOTIFY: a notification of "sample.private.data" reaches the
    subscribers of "sample.private" and "sample" as well, so the authorizer is
    asked about the subject and each of its ancestors, and the notification is
    dropped if it refuses any of them. A client therefore cannot notify the
    subscribers of a protected subject through a subject below it.


!!! Security Considerations

A browser does not restrict a WebSocket connection to the origin of the page
that opens it, and it sends the user's cookies with the upgrade request. Any
page the user visits can therefore try to connect to the WebEventService, with
the session of the user. The following is checked before a connection is
established:

  - The Origin header. An upgrade request whose Origin is neither the
    request's own nor one of the origins listed in
    osp.web.event.allowedOrigins is rejected with status 403. The request's
    own origin has the scheme of the connection (https for a secure one),
    and the host and port of the Host header. Requests without an Origin
    header come from clients other than browsers and are accepted. The web
    server checks the Origin before it authenticates the request (the
    allowWebSocketOrigin attribute of the extension), so that a refused
    request neither uses nor extends the user's session; the service checks
    it again, which also covers an empty permission.
  - The credentials (see Authentication and Authorization above).
  - The number of connections, if osp.web.event.maxWebSockets is set.

An application that must restrict access further registers a delegate for the
requestReceived event, which is fired for every upgrade request that passed the
checks above. The delegate can inspect the request and reject the connection by
setting the status of the response to anything other than 200 (OK).

Without a subject authorizer (see above), a connection that has been admitted may
subscribe to every subject. Credentials are checked once, on the upgrade
request: a connection stays open after the session it was opened with has
ended, or after the user has lost the permission.

A connection belongs to the user that the web server has authenticated for
its upgrade request, to no user if a bearer token with a scope authenticated
it, or, if a resolver is installed with setUserResolver(), to the user the
resolver gives, and notifyUser() delivers only to the connections of that
user. The resolver runs for every connection that requestReceived has
accepted; it identifies the user but rejects nothing.

The page reported by listSubscribers() is taken from the request's "page"
query parameter or its Referer header. It is supplied by the client, is not
checked, and must be escaped before it is shown in a page.

Clients cannot send notifications to other clients unless
osp.web.event.clientNotify is set to true. When it is set, any connected
client can notify any subject, and the delegates of notificationSent and
subjectNotified cannot tell such a notification from one made by notify().

Event messages reach every client that has subscribed to their subject. The
following guidelines should be observed:

  - Do not notify a subject with data that not every user who may connect is
    allowed to see. Use notifyUser() for data meant for one user only.
  - Use cryptographic hashes or tokens to refer to actual data. For example, when notifying
    a client that a specific data object on the server has been modified, and the client
    needs to update its representation of it, send a cryptographic hash identifying the object
    in the event message. Only authorized clients already in possession of the hash can then
    associate the event with the object, and the event message is meaningless to everyone else.


!!! Delivery Semantics and Flow Control

Notifications are delivered to each subscriber through a per-subscriber send queue,
drained by a pool of worker threads (see osp.web.event.workers). A worker sends one
frame at a time and waits while the subscriber's connection does not take it. While
fewer subscribers are stalled than there are workers, the other subscribers are served
without delay. When every worker is held by a stalled subscriber, delivery to all
subscribers waits until one of those subscribers is disconnected, which takes at most
about eleven seconds: the send timeout of ten seconds, checked once a second.

Delivery to a subscriber that cannot keep up is lossy:

  - While a frame for a subject is still queued for a subscriber, a newer notification
    for the same subject replaces the queued frame (latest value wins). Frames are
    always delivered in the order the notifications were made.
  - A subscriber with overlapping subscriptions (e.g., to a subject and one of its
    ancestors) receives a single frame per notification.
  - The number of queued frames per subscriber is limited by the
    osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames configuration property. When the limit is
    exceeded, the oldest queued notification frame for that subscriber is dropped
    and a warning is logged. The keepalive PING and the PONG that answers the
    client's latest PING are never dropped; at most one of each is queued.
  - A subscriber to which a frame has not been sent completely within ten seconds
    is disconnected and removed, within about another second. The limit applies
    to the whole frame, so a client that reads a few bytes at a time cannot hold
    a worker longer.

NOTE: On Windows, a blocking send of a frame to a subscriber that does not read
completes at once: the system takes the whole frame, of any size, into its kernel
buffers. There, neither osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames nor the send timeout limits
the memory that such frames take.

In the other direction, a client that has started a frame and does not send
it completely within ten seconds is disconnected and removed as well, within
about another second.

When a subscriber is removed, for whatever reason, its connection is closed within
two seconds, within about another second. The limit applies to a frame still being
sent to or received from the subscriber, the CLOSE frame of the server and the
client's confirmation of the close together; when it is reached, the connection is
closed without the confirmation.

Server-side events (notificationSent, subjectNotified) are not affected by any of
this and fire for every notification.


!!! Configuring the WebEventService

The WebEventService provides the following configuration properties in the global
application configuration.


!! osp.web.event.maxWebSockets

Limit the number of WebSocket connections (and thus connected clients).
Specify 0 for an unlimited (as far as system resources allow) number of connections.
Defaults to 0 (unlimited).


!! osp.web.event.maxSubjects

The maximum number of subjects one connection can subscribe to. A SUBSCRIBE
message beyond the limit closes the connection. Specify 0 for no limit.
Defaults to 64.


!! osp.web.event.workers

The number of worker threads used for sending and receiving WebSocket frames,
at most 64. Defaults to two times the number of processor cores.


!! osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames

The maximum number of outbound frames queued per subscriber.
When a subscriber cannot keep up and the limit is exceeded, the oldest queued
notification frame for that subscriber is dropped. The keepalive PING and the
PONG that answers the client's latest PING, at most one of each, count against
the limit but are never dropped, so with a limit of 1 two frames can be queued.
Specify 0 for unlimited queueing (which can exhaust memory if clients stall).
Defaults to 256.


!! osp.web.event.session

The name of the web session whose cookie authenticates the WebSocket upgrade
requests of browsers. A browser is admitted only once it has logged in to that
session: the session stores the name of the user in its "username" property, and
the user is an account of the auth service. Defaults to empty: no session login;
every client authenticates the upgrade request with an Authorization header,
with HTTP Basic credentials, or with a bearer token if the web server has a
token validator (osp.web.tokenValidatorName). The global osp.web.authMethods
setting limits which of the three apply.

A bundle property websession.id is not read: the bundle logs an error when it is
set.


!! osp.web.event.permission

The permission a client needs to open a WebEvent connection. The web server
checks it on the WebSocket upgrade request, before the connection is
established; a request without valid credentials is answered with 401
Unauthorized, one of a user without the permission with 403 Forbidden, and,
while no auth service (osp.web.authServiceName) is registered, every request
with 503 Service Unavailable, except as described for "**" below. "*" admits
every authenticated user of an existing account and nobody else. A permission name, or a comma-separated
list of names (any one of which suffices), admits only the users granted it.
There is no permission per subject: a connection that is admitted may subscribe
to every subject that the subject authorizer, if one is installed, allows.
Defaults to "*".

An empty value turns the authentication of the upgrade request off: the web
server does not authenticate the request, also when it carries credentials,
and the connection has no user unless a user resolver (setUserResolver())
gives it one. The service still checks the Origin header, and a delegate of the
requestReceived event decides whether the connection is accepted. This admits
unauthenticated connections; it is meant for applications that authenticate
their clients themselves, for example by a token in the query of the upgrade
request, and works without an auth service.

"**" is not a substitute for an empty value. It admits a request without
credentials, but it answers a request with credentials that the web server
cannot check (another authorization scheme, or a bearer token without a token
validator) with 401, and a request with credentials or the cookie of a
logged-in session with 503 while no auth service is registered.

A bundle property permission is not read: the bundle logs an error when it is
set, and does not start if it is set and osp.web.event.permission is not.


!! osp.web.event.allowedOrigins

A comma-separated list of origins (for example "https://app.example.com") from
which browsers may connect, in addition to the origin of the service itself.
Defaults to an empty list.

Note that behind a proxy that terminates TLS, the service sees plain HTTP
connections, and the public origin of the pages (for example
"https://app.example.com") must be listed here.

The extension of the bundle passes the list to the web server as well
(allowWebSocketOrigin="${osp.web.event.allowedOrigins:-}"), so with a
permission set, the web server refuses an upgrade from an origin that is not
allowed before the service sees it, and logs the refusal at debug level only.


!! osp.web.event.clientNotify

Set to true to pass NOTIFY messages sent by clients on to the other
subscribers and to the server's delegates. Defaults to false, which drops such
messages.


!! osp.web.event.pingInterval

The keepalive sweep interval in seconds. Every interval, subscribers that have
not sent anything for at least half the interval are sent a WebSocket PING
control frame (browsers answer with a PONG automatically), and subscribers that
have stayed silent for twice the interval are considered dead and removed.
This reclaims connections whose peer disappeared without closing the socket
(network drop, suspend, power loss). Specify 0 to disable the keepalive sweep.
Defaults to 60.
